{
  "wireMagic": "pb3",
  "pens": [ {
  
  } ],
  "wsWinStyles": [ {
  
  } ],
  "wpWinPositions": [ {
  
  } ],
  "events": [ {
    "tStartMs": 80,
    "dDurationMs": 5600,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "In order to make a nice, clear ice cube for your drinks, it’s important to consider quantum fields.  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 5680,
    "dDurationMs": 5520,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "First, boil to release dissolved gasses, then make sure the freezing extends through the cube  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 11200,
    "dDurationMs": 6560,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "from a single surface. If the crystallization \nprocess starts from multiple nucleation points  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 17760,
    "dDurationMs": 4720,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "then there’ll be imperfections in the lattice \nstructure where the regions of spreading ice  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 22480,
    "dDurationMs": 9360,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "meet - what we call topological defects. So where do quantum fields come into all of this? Well, it turns out the  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 31840,
    "dDurationMs": 6320,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "universe is a gigantic ice cube, and the imperfect freezing of its quantum fields right after the  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 38160,
    "dDurationMs": 7200,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Big Bang very likely left vast topological \ndefects stretching across the sky. These are  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 45360,
    "dDurationMs": 7528,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "cosmic strings, and many physicists think that they have to exist, and that we can find them."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 58160,
    "dDurationMs": 5600,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Reality has cracks in it. Universe-spanning \nfilaments of ancient Big Bang energy,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 63760,
    "dDurationMs": 6560,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "formed from topological defects in the quantum fields, aka cosmic strings. They have subatomic  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 70320,
    "dDurationMs": 5981,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "thickness but prodigious mass and they lash \nthrough space at a close to the speed of light.  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 76301,
    "dDurationMs": 7299,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "They could be the most bizarre undiscovered entities that probably actually exist. To understand cosmic  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 83600,
    "dDurationMs": 6400,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "strings, and to convince you that they probably do exist, we need to understand phase transitions  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 90000,
    "dDurationMs": 6960,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "in quantum fields - we need to see how a whole \nuniverse can freeze like a badly-made ice cube. \n  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 96960,
    "dDurationMs": 7520,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Heat up ice and it melts, keep heating the water \nand it vaporizes, more heat still and that water vapor  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 104480,
    "dDurationMs": 5600,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "ionizes into plasma. But that’s not the final \nphase transition. Keep heating until you hit  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 110080,
    "dDurationMs": 6000,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "temperatures of the extremely early universe and a \nphase transition occurs in the quantum fields that  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 116080,
    "dDurationMs": 6480,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "underlie all particles. Just as with water, a \nfield’s inherent temperature massively changes its  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 122560,
    "dDurationMs": 5600,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "behavior. For example, the force-carrying field of \nthe modern universe has a complicated structure.  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 128160,
    "dDurationMs": 5760,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "There are many different ways it can vibrate. \nThese modes manifest as different force-carrying  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 133920,
    "dDurationMs": 6320,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "particles moving in what we think of as separate \nforce fields. This gives us our familiar  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 140240,
    "dDurationMs": 5680,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "electromagnetic, strong and weak nuclear forces. But at very high temperatures,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 145920,
    "dDurationMs": 6560,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "the complexities of the quantum fields sort of \nget ironed out, a little like how the complex  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 152480,
    "dDurationMs": 6480,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "crystal structure of ice dissolves when it melts. \nIt seems pretty certain that in the first searing  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 158960,
    "dDurationMs": 6000,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "instant after the big bang, most of the modes \nof vibration of the quantum fields vanished.  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 164960,
    "dDurationMs": 6720,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "The many force-carrying fields behaved as a \nsingle field, generating a single master force.  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 171680,
    "dDurationMs": 6320,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "We know for sure that this is true of the \nelectromagnetic and weak nuclear forces - we’ve  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 178000,
    "dDurationMs": 6400,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "re-merged those in our particle colliders - \nbut it’s almost certainly the case for the  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 184400,
    "dDurationMs": 6720,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "strong nuclear force and the Higgs field also. That’s right, I said Higgs field. We think of the  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 191120,
    "dDurationMs": 6480,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Higgs field and Higgs boson as giving elementary particles their masses, but we should also think of  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 197600,
    "dDurationMs": 6240,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "the Higgs as a fifth fundamental force, because \nit arises from the same field structure as the  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 203840,
    "dDurationMs": 7440,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "other non-gravity forces. And it’s the freezing of this field that can give us our cosmic strings.\n  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 211280,
    "dDurationMs": 5360,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Now a quantum field is just some numerical \nproperty that the fabric of space can have.  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 216640,
    "dDurationMs": 5920,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "The field at any point can oscillate around that value, and those oscillations can have  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 222560,
    "dDurationMs": 6880,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "quantized energy states. These vibrations can move through space, and we see them as particles.  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 229440,
    "dDurationMs": 3920,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "A field’s numerical value is called \nits field strength and it depends on  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 233360,
    "dDurationMs": 6880,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "the amount of energy in the field, sometimes \nin complex ways. In the absence of particles,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 240240,
    "dDurationMs": 6320,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "a field will always drop to the nearest minimum \nin energy - this is the vacuum state of the field.  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 246560,
    "dDurationMs": 5920,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "In the early universe, the Higgs field had \na very simple response to changes in energy,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 252480,
    "dDurationMs": 6880,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "with a single minimum value, and even this \nvacuum state still contained a lot of energy.\n  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 259360,
    "dDurationMs": 5040,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "The shape of this so-called potential curve \ndepends on the temperature. As the universe  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 264400,
    "dDurationMs": 6240,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "expanded and things cooled down, the Higgs \nfield potential developed a bump. The lowest  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 270640,
    "dDurationMs": 6560,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "energy value was no longer a single number - \ninstead new minima appeared around the old value.  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 277200,
    "dDurationMs": 5840,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Actually, the Higgs field is really characterized \nby two numbers - a pair of field strengths, and so  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 283040,
    "dDurationMs": 8240,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "the new minimum formed a ring around the old minimum, \nresembling an item of festive Mexican headwear."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 291280,
    "dDurationMs": 4080,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "So, quite suddenly the Higgs \nfield everywhere in the universe  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 295360,
    "dDurationMs": 7120,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "found itself sitting at a higher energy than it \nneeded. It was momentarily stable at that point,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 302480,
    "dDurationMs": 6000,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "just like a ball sitting at the top of a hill. But \nthe slightest quantum jiggle would send the ball,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 308480,
    "dDurationMs": 7120,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "or the Higgs, rolling down in a random direction. \nAnd that’s what happened. Here and there across  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 315600,
    "dDurationMs": 6320,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "the universe, the Higgs field started falling \ntowards the new vacuum state - we call this vacuum  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 321920,
    "dDurationMs": 6480,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "decay. Neighboring points in a field drag on each \nother, pulling them towards the same value, just  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 328400,
    "dDurationMs": 6560,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "like how the magnetic dipoles in a ferromagnet \ndrag each other into alignment. So, when vacuum  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 334960,
    "dDurationMs": 6800,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "decay started at one point neighboring points were \ndragged to the same part of the Higgs minimum.  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 341760,
    "dDurationMs": 4880,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "A bubble of this lower vacuum energy was \nnucleated, and it expanded at the speed  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 346640,
    "dDurationMs": 4880,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "of light. Many bubbles would have started \nat different places across the universe,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 351520,
    "dDurationMs": 7554,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "and when the bubbles found each other and merged, \nthe old, high-energy vacuum was completely erased."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 359074,
    "dDurationMs": 7406,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Or mostly erased. Just as with ice, topological \ndefects should have formed where these bubbles  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 366480,
    "dDurationMs": 6960,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "met. Our ice cube forms sheets, but our \nHiggs field formed strings. Remember  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 373440,
    "dDurationMs": 4160,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "that the vacuum decayed in a random \ndirection towards this circular valley.  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 377600,
    "dDurationMs": 4880,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "That means we can ascribe an angle to \nevery point in space defining the relative  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 382480,
    "dDurationMs": 5680,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "value of the two components of the Higgs \nfield. We’ll call that the phase angle. \n  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 388160,
    "dDurationMs": 5840,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Across a single expanding region of decaying vacuum, the phase angle would have been similar  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 394000,
    "dDurationMs": 6080,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "because these points were all pulled in the \ndirection of the initial nucleation event. But  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 400080,
    "dDurationMs": 6800,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "independent bubbles may have very different phase angles. When bubbles met, the Higgs phase angle at  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 406880,
    "dDurationMs": 6480,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "the boundary tried to rotate to line up. This led to textures of slowly shifting phase angles across  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 413360,
    "dDurationMs": 6400,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "the universe. But if multiple bubbles join with \ndifferent phase angles then sometimes the lowest  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 419760,
    "dDurationMs": 5600,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "energy approach to lining up the phase angles \nis for them to vary smoothly around a loop - a  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 425360,
    "dDurationMs": 7600,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "2-pi rotation of the phase angle around the \nintersection. And that left a knot somewhere  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 432960,
    "dDurationMs": 6320,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "inside the loop where the fields couldn’t align. \nThe Higgs field at the center of that knot  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 439280,
    "dDurationMs": 7120,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "was forced to take on the Higgs value at the top \nof the potential hill rather than the valley. It  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 446400,
    "dDurationMs": 7120,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "became a fossil of the ancient, high-energy vacuum \nthat would persists into the modern universe."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 453520,
    "dDurationMs": 7360,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "This sort of swirly topological defect is called \na vortex, and we see 2-D versions everywhere from  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 460880,
    "dDurationMs": 8560,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "cyclones to swirls of hair on your head. But \nin a 3-D space, like, you know, actual space,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 469440,
    "dDurationMs": 5360,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "this sort of defect manifests as a \ncylindrical swirl around a central line.  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 474800,
    "dDurationMs": 5280,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "And that central line is our cosmic string. \n \nOther topological defects may be possible.  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 480080,
    "dDurationMs": 5920,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "For example, a zero-dimensional, point-like \ntopological defect would be a magnetic monopoles,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 486000,
    "dDurationMs": 6160,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "which we talked about recently. There are also \nways to produce 2-D defects called domain walls,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 492160,
    "dDurationMs": 4160,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "but that’s for another time. OK, so we’ve managed to freeze the  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 496320,
    "dDurationMs": 6560,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "quantum fields amidst the first bawlings of the baby universe and woven some cosmic strings.  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 502880,
    "dDurationMs": 7360,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "What do they look like and what do they do? Those \nphase angles really do prefer to line up, which  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 510240,
    "dDurationMs": 7680,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "means the loops around the defect tighten as much \nas they can. The filament of high vacuum energy is  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 517920,
    "dDurationMs": 6640,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "squeezed it down to one-ten-trillionth the width \nof a proton. And yet it still holds an incredible  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 524560,
    "dDurationMs": 6160,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "amount of energy, which gives it the mass of the planet Mars for every 100 meters of length."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 530720,
    "dDurationMs": 6080,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "And these things are long. They started as long \nas light can travel between the nucleation event  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 536800,
    "dDurationMs": 6000,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "and the completion of vacuum decay and then the \nexpanding universe stretched them up to the size  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 542800,
    "dDurationMs": 7280,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "of the observable universe. We actually expect \nmultiple nucleation events in each causal horizon,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 550080,
    "dDurationMs": 5040,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "potentially leading to dozens of cosmic \nstrings in a network across the universe.\n  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 555120,
    "dDurationMs": 5360,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Unlike the topological defects in ice, \ncosmic strings move and vibrate. They are  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 560480,
    "dDurationMs": 6480,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "also under pretty insane tension, so vibrations \ntravel along them at near the speed of light.  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 566960,
    "dDurationMs": 4320,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "This inevitably leads to collisions \nbetween segments of strings–either  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 571280,
    "dDurationMs": 5200,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "two distinct strings or two sections of the \nsame string. When this happens either the  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 576480,
    "dDurationMs": 6080,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "two segments pass straight through each other, \nor they switch partners - they intercommute.\n  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 582560,
    "dDurationMs": 5840,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "If a straight string collides with itself it can \ncut out a loop. Then, if the loop intersects with  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 588400,
    "dDurationMs": 7840,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "itself again, it forms two smaller loops, chopping \nup into smaller and smaller loops. But larger and  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 596240,
    "dDurationMs": 6720,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "larger loops keep forming from the original giant \ncosmic strings. Over time, the size of the largest  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 602960,
    "dDurationMs": 6480,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "loops increases, while at the same time populating \nthe universe with their chopped-up offspring."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 609440,
    "dDurationMs": 7920,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Once intercommutation occurs, a pair of “kinks” is formed in each of the newly formed strings speed  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 617360,
    "dDurationMs": 5360,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "away from each other along the string at near the \nspeed of light. They’re whipped back and forth by  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 622720,
    "dDurationMs": 5920,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "the oscillating string, and the incredible mass in the kinks causes them to radiate  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 628640,
    "dDurationMs": 7360,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "gravitational waves. In this way cosmic strings \nshed energy, and so they slowly decay away.  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 636000,
    "dDurationMs": 5360,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Eventually they vanish as the Higgs field smooths \nitself out across the filament. The smaller the  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 641360,
    "dDurationMs": 7520,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "loop size the quicker they evaporate, so the \nbreaking up of loops accelerates their demise.\n  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 648880,
    "dDurationMs": 7360,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "OK, that’s what cosmic strings do. Now, how do we \nfind them, assuming they exist? Well let’s start  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 656240,
    "dDurationMs": 5360,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "with these gravitational waves. That radiation \nshould be emitted in beams in the direction of  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 661600,
    "dDurationMs": 6160,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "oscillation of the string, so we might see flashes \nas these beams pass over our gravitational wave  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 667760,
    "dDurationMs": 6720,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "observatories. These are likely too weak to be seen at our current detectors such as LIGO,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 674480,
    "dDurationMs": 5440,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "but future detectors such as LISA might be \nsensitive enough. Then there’s the Pulsar  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 679920,
    "dDurationMs": 5680,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Timing Array - as we’ve described previously, \nit detects gravitational waves by looking for  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 685600,
    "dDurationMs": 6240,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "irregularities in the period of the fantastically \nregular flashes of light from pulsars.  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 691840,
    "dDurationMs": 7856,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "It also has the potential to spot the tell-tale signals from gravitationally radiating kinks in cosmic strings. \n  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 699696,
    "dDurationMs": 5504,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "The other way to spot cosmic strings also \nrelies on a gravitational effect: gravitational  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 705200,
    "dDurationMs": 7360,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "lensing - which is the warping of background light sources due to the space-time warping effect of gravity.  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 712560,
    "dDurationMs": 5120,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "When a massive object sits between \nus and a distant light source, it bends all  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 717680,
    "dDurationMs": 7360,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "passing rays of light inwards, so focusing them \ntowards us. We can see multiple images or even a  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 725040,
    "dDurationMs": 6960,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "ring surrounding that lens. A cosmic string would also deflect light towards itself, but that can  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 732000,
    "dDurationMs": 7920,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "only lead to a pair of split images, and that could potential leave a chain of split images across the sky."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 739920,
    "dDurationMs": 5680,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "No such chain has yet been detected, \nbut upcoming gigantic all-sky surveys  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 745600,
    "dDurationMs": 5760,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "may give us the data that we need to find these. Now if we do find a cosmic string, there’s one  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 751360,
    "dDurationMs": 6240,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "other point of confusion we’d need to settle. \nIs this a cosmic string, or is it a cosmic  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 757600,
    "dDurationMs": 5840,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "superstring? You’ve probably heard of string \ntheory - we’ve certainly talked about it enough  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 763440,
    "dDurationMs": 5920,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "on this show. It’s perhaps the most established \ncandidate for a theory of everything - a theory  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 769360,
    "dDurationMs": 4720,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "that brings together all physics as we know it. \nThe fundamental building blocks of the theory are  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 774080,
    "dDurationMs": 5440,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "these subatomic 1-dimensional filaments called, \nfittingly, strings. The strings of string theory  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 779520,
    "dDurationMs": 5280,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "have nothing to do with the cosmic strings that I described. For one thing, they’re ridiculously  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 784800,
    "dDurationMs": 6800,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "tiny instead of universe-sized. However the \nuniverse may have found a way to confuse the two.\n  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 791600,
    "dDurationMs": 6720,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Many physicists think that in the extremely early \nuniverse the so-called inflationary epoch expanded  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 798320,
    "dDurationMs": 6080,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "the subatomic into the cosmic. Some of these \nstring-theoretic strings may have been stretched  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 804400,
    "dDurationMs": 6800,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "to universe-size by that event. Now those are called cosmic superstrings, and annoyingly they behave  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 811200,
    "dDurationMs": 5600,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "like “regular” cosmic strings in many ways - \nlike the gravitational waves and the lensing.\n  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 816800,
    "dDurationMs": 6000,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "But there are differences. While cosmic strings \nalmost always intercommute when they collide,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 822800,
    "dDurationMs": 4400,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "cosmic superstrings are far more likely \nto pass straight through each other,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 827200,
    "dDurationMs": 6800,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "which reduces the rate of chopping up. They can also form junctions, specifically where two  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 834000,
    "dDurationMs": 5200,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "different types of superstring meet and combine \nto form a third, connected string which is,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 839200,
    "dDurationMs": 5520,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "in a sense, a combination of the two. This gives us a potential way to distinguish our  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 844720,
    "dDurationMs": 6400,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "cosmic string-type. If one of these superstring \njunctions does any gravitational lensing,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 851120,
    "dDurationMs": 6651,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "it should produce a six-part image, perhaps \nwith a parade of split pairs approaching it.  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 857771,
    "dDurationMs": 6309,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Observation of such a junction would be the \nbest - dare I say only - evidence to date in  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 864080,
    "dDurationMs": 4400,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "support of string theory. We also expect \ncosmic superstrings to decay less quickly  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 868480,
    "dDurationMs": 4480,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "because they don’t chop into loops as fast. \nThat means they should result in a stronger  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 872960,
    "dDurationMs": 6320,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "gravitational wave background, and possibly \na distinct gravitational wave signature.\n  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 879280,
    "dDurationMs": 6560,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Now we haven’t actually found cosmic strings or superstrings … yet. But our searches  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 885840,
    "dDurationMs": 6400,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "have given us bounds on the range of allowed \ntensions–and therefore energies–of these things.  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 892240,
    "dDurationMs": 6000,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "And we have to keep looking, because it’s very \npossible that the universe is riddled with veins  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 898240,
    "dDurationMs": 7440,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "of its primordial vacuum. If we can find one who \nknows what we’ll learn? We may discover truths  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 905680,
    "dDurationMs": 5040,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "about the origins of the universe, or the nature \nof quantum fields, or the validity of string  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 910720,
    "dDurationMs": 8080,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "theory. Many murky mysteries may become as clear \nas a well-made ice cube. I mean, what better way  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 918800,
    "dDurationMs": 7040,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "to see its inner workings of the universe than \nto find a crack in the fabric of spacetime."
    } ]
  } ]
}
